{"id":3745,"date":"2021-09-24T09:24:44","date_gmt":"2021-09-24T16:24:44","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=3745"},"modified":"2023-08-21T08:35:50","modified_gmt":"2023-08-21T15:35:50","slug":"4-4-height","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/4-4-height\/","title":{"rendered":"4.4: Height"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214b69df8f\" name=\"6a2214b69df8f\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Goals and Steps to Success<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Goals:<\/p>\n<p>By the end of this lesson, I will be able to:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Explain how the get_height() function abstracts the calculations I performed in the previous lesson<\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Explain how the drone measures height using barometer values<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Check the drone&#8217;s height at various points in a program<\/span><\/li>\n<li>Store the drone&#8217;s height as a variable and use it to make decisions<\/li>\n<li>Identify variances or differences from the drone&#8217;s measured height versus its actual height<\/li>\n<li>Explain why the drone&#8217;s program height may vary from reality<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Steps to Success:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>How High in the Sky?<\/li>\n<li>Takeoff Height<\/li>\n<li>Measuring Height in a Loop<\/li>\n<li>Challenge 1: Fact Checker<\/li>\n<li>Challenge 2: Flight Ceiling<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214b69e0ce\" name=\"6a2214b69e0ce\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>How High in the Sky?<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Quick review: the barometer in your CoDrone Mini measures the surrounding air pressure. Generally, air pressure is greatest at sea level and then decreases as the altitude, or height, increases.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">With your CoDrone Mini, you will be using the barometer to check sea level and then the altitude from the floor. This can be done once or multiple times in one program.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">The function that you will be using to measure the height of your drone from the floor is \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">get_height()<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">. This function checks the barometer while on the ground and then the value in the air and returns a height in centimeters. Very similar to what you did in the challenge from the previous lesson!<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Try out this example program to see what it looks like in action!\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\n\r\ndrone.hover(3)\r\n\r\nheight = drone.get_height()\r\n\r\nprint(height)\r\n\r\ndrone.land()\r\n\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214b69e152\" name=\"6a2214b69e152\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Takeoff Height<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Get the height of your drone before takeoff, while it\u2019s in the air, and after it lands!<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">After importing the library, creating the drone object, and pairing with CoDrone Mini, create a variable that will get the drone\u2019s height on the floor.<\/span> <span style=\"font-weight: 400\">We named ours\u00a0 <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">begin_height<\/code><\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">. <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Write a takeoff command after it.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Next, create another variable that will get the drone\u2019s height while it is in the air (ours is \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">mid_height<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">) and include a land command after that. Create one more variable that will get the drone\u2019s height after landing.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Finally, write three print commands that will display the drone\u2019s heights at each part of its flight.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">This is what your program should look like:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\nbegin_height = drone.get_height()\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\n\r\nmid_height = drone.get_height()\r\n\r\ndrone.land()\r\n\r\nend_height = drone.get_height()\r\n\r\n\r\nprint(\"Before taking off: \", begin_height)\r\n\r\nprint(\"After taking off: \", mid_height)\r\n\r\nprint(\"Landed: \", end_height)\r\n\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Once you\u2019ve seen this, play around with your program! What happens if you move the print statements around or add more flight movements?<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214b69e1e3\" name=\"6a2214b69e1e3\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Measuring Height in a Loop<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">The \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">get_height()<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">function can be used in a loop to measure one height after the other. In this program, you\u2019re going to measure the altitude of your drone as it keeps throttling up.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">To start, import the CoDrone Mini and time libraries, create the drone object, and then pair to CoDrone Mini. Next, create a variable called<\/span> \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">throttle<\/code>\u00a0<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">and set it to 20, which is its power percentage. Then reset your drone\u2019s barometer.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Include a print command that will print the drone\u2019s initial height, which should be the floor. Next, create a list called \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">height_data<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">. This will store all of the altitudes that your drone reaches.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Set up a for loop that repeats three times. Your drone should takeoff and throttle up for 1.5 seconds at the percentage stored in the throttle variable, which starts out at 20. The \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">get_height()<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">function should then get the altitude and add that value to the \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">height_data()<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">list. The drone should then land and then sleep for 5 seconds, which will give you time to reposition it if needed. Next, a value of 10 should be added to <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">throttle<\/code>.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">After the loop, include a command to print the data that was stored in the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">height_data()<\/code><\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">list. Does the data look like what you expected?<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Your program should look something like this:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\nimport time\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\nthrottle = 20\r\n\r\ndrone.reset_sensor()\r\n\r\ndrone.set_trim(25,15) # set your own trim values\r\n\r\ntime.sleep(1) # to avoid skipping takeoff()\r\n\r\nprint(\"Initial: \", drone.get_height())\r\n\r\nheight_data = []\r\n\r\nfor i in range(3):\r\n\r\n    drone.takeoff()\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_throttle(throttle)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.move(1.5)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 height_data.append(drone.get_height())\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.land()\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 time.sleep(5) # gives time to reposition your drone on the ground\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 throttle = throttle + 10\r\n\r\nprint(height_data)\r\n\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214b69e234\" name=\"6a2214b69e234\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge 1: Fact Checker<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Have CoDrone Mini fly to three different places in the room you\u2019re in, like to the top of a closet, the top of a bookcase, and the top of a chair. Include print commands so you know how many centimeters the altitude is at each location. Next, get a meter stick or measuring tape and see if the height of each place matches up with what was printed out!<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214b69e297\" name=\"6a2214b69e297\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge 2: Flight Ceiling<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Commercial and recreational aircraft often operate under a &#8220;service ceiling&#8221;, this is the maximum supported altitude for the aircraft. For example, our drone&#8217;s typically aren&#8217;t flying as high as commercial airlines. This is because each aircraft&#8217;s design determines how high up it can actually fly. For this challenge, we want to create a service ceiling for our drone in the program. To do this, follow the steps below:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Create a variable called SERVICE_CEILING and set it to a value of your choosing in centimeters. We use all caps, because this is a\u00a0<strong>CONSTANT<\/strong>, meaning its value should not change during a program.<\/li>\n<li>Define a function that has no parameters but checks the height against the SERVICE_CEILING constant. If the drone is above the ceiling, it should blink the LED red and land the drone. If not, it should continue on in the program.<\/li>\n<li>Write some flight commands that will eventually force the drone close to or above the SERVICE_CEILING to test your limiting of the drone&#8217;s flight.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 7 Goals and Steps to Success [vc_column_text]Goals: By the end of this lesson, I will be able to: Explain how the get_height() function abstracts the calculations I performed&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/4-4-height\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":4147,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":3473,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1782,1848,1770,1793,1826,1482,853,694,59,171,63],"lesson-tag":[2159,2125,2160,2156,2122,2134,2155],"class_list":["post-3745","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-barometer","lesson-tag-codrone-mini","lesson-tag-height","lesson-tag-intermediate","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-python","lesson-tag-sensor","module-level-4","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/3745","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":9,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/3745\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":4509,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/3745\/revisions\/4509"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/4147"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=3745"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=3745"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=3745"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}